WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.517
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.517 --> 00:00:08.100
ZOLTAN ARATO: My
name is Zoltan Arato,

00:00:08.100 --> 00:00:12.360
I'm a product manager
of Google Cloud IT Core.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:14.580
Let me also introduce
Preston Holmes--

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:16.530
head of IoT Solutions--

00:00:16.530 --> 00:00:18.000
for Google also.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.720
He's going to be on
stage with us shortly.

00:00:21.720 --> 00:00:25.920
So, today we are going to talk
about a specific type of IoT

00:00:25.920 --> 00:00:27.740
applications.

00:00:27.740 --> 00:00:33.930
Applications those involve,
of course, IoT devices,

00:00:33.930 --> 00:00:38.190
mobile or web applications,
and-- very importantly-- also,

00:00:38.190 --> 00:00:39.450
users.

00:00:39.450 --> 00:00:42.120
And you are going
to see that users

00:00:42.120 --> 00:00:46.960
will be an important component
in this presentation.

00:00:46.960 --> 00:00:49.750
And as part of
this presentation,

00:00:49.750 --> 00:00:52.800
we will review, sort of,
the theoretical background,

00:00:52.800 --> 00:00:54.730
the business context.

00:00:54.730 --> 00:00:59.070
And then we will show you a
tool box using Cloud IoT Core.

00:00:59.070 --> 00:01:01.290
And some of the Firebase
product, how you could

00:01:01.290 --> 00:01:04.140
implement these use-cases.

00:01:04.140 --> 00:01:08.930
And we will finish
with the demo.

00:01:08.930 --> 00:01:13.460
So, both Preston and
I, have been working in

00:01:13.460 --> 00:01:19.070
around IoT projects for
almost five years, now.

00:01:19.070 --> 00:01:23.600
And we have seen
two main patterns

00:01:23.600 --> 00:01:27.780
emerging when it comes
to IoT to use-cases.

00:01:27.780 --> 00:01:29.270
The first one is--

00:01:29.270 --> 00:01:31.010
I like to call them--

00:01:31.010 --> 00:01:37.040
data ingestion intensive, or
data ingestion heavy use-cases.

00:01:37.040 --> 00:01:40.970
In these use-cases,
there are many devices

00:01:40.970 --> 00:01:44.000
sending a lot of data upstream.

00:01:44.000 --> 00:01:47.750
So, typically, the bandwidth
requirement upstream

00:01:47.750 --> 00:01:50.900
is much higher than downstream.

00:01:50.900 --> 00:01:53.690
And usually, these
are applications

00:01:53.690 --> 00:01:56.300
for monitoring or analytics.

00:01:56.300 --> 00:01:58.670
And I mentioned
that users will be

00:01:58.670 --> 00:02:00.720
an important part of our talk.

00:02:00.720 --> 00:02:04.310
And in these use-cases,
usually there

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:07.350
are less personas involved.

00:02:07.350 --> 00:02:11.170
On the other hand, the
other type of application--

00:02:11.170 --> 00:02:14.900
we usually see when
working with customers--

00:02:14.900 --> 00:02:18.860
we like to call it complex
application logic use-cases.

00:02:18.860 --> 00:02:23.240
So, in these use-cases--
or applications--

00:02:23.240 --> 00:02:25.400
the data model is
usually more complex.

00:02:28.130 --> 00:02:29.960
There is a similar need--

00:02:29.960 --> 00:02:32.060
in terms of bandwidth
requirements--

00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:34.160
upstream and downstream.

00:02:34.160 --> 00:02:37.670
And that's because these
applications are not

00:02:37.670 --> 00:02:42.150
only about receiving
data from devices,

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:44.850
but also controlling them.

00:02:44.850 --> 00:02:47.690
So, most of the time, this
complex business logic

00:02:47.690 --> 00:02:51.050
is used to determine some sort
of change that is required

00:02:51.050 --> 00:02:52.760
to be performed on the devices.

00:02:52.760 --> 00:02:55.940
And then sent back these
commands or configurations

00:02:55.940 --> 00:02:57.380
to the devices.

00:02:57.380 --> 00:03:00.950
And what makes the
implementation of these

00:03:00.950 --> 00:03:08.450
use-cases challenging is
the multiple personas--

00:03:08.450 --> 00:03:10.460
sort of in the mix--
or having to interact

00:03:10.460 --> 00:03:13.980
with these deployments.

00:03:13.980 --> 00:03:17.130
So, when it comes to these
complex application logic use

00:03:17.130 --> 00:03:21.660
cases, typically these
are the building blocks

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:26.410
that we see as required
for implementation.

00:03:26.410 --> 00:03:30.660
So, mobile and web applications
to control the devices

00:03:30.660 --> 00:03:33.200
and see their actual state.

00:03:33.200 --> 00:03:35.370
The devices, of course.

00:03:35.370 --> 00:03:38.970
Device management
capabilities meaning

00:03:38.970 --> 00:03:44.400
like, Credential Management,
grouping, updating

00:03:44.400 --> 00:03:47.070
the firmware, of the devices.

00:03:47.070 --> 00:03:51.300
Cloud services, data
management tools in the Cloud.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:55.410
Or storage solutions
or computing solutions.

00:03:55.410 --> 00:04:00.630
User management, so, most
commonly, an identity

00:04:00.630 --> 00:04:05.770
provider to store your users
and then handle your users.

00:04:05.770 --> 00:04:08.530
We already touched upon
command and control.

00:04:08.530 --> 00:04:13.200
So, the capability to interact
with the devices, to be able to

00:04:13.200 --> 00:04:14.860
control them.

00:04:14.860 --> 00:04:17.670
And last-- but not
least-- sensor data

00:04:17.670 --> 00:04:21.810
aggregation and visualization.

00:04:21.810 --> 00:04:22.980
So you want to see--

00:04:22.980 --> 00:04:26.190
or in these use-cases,
users want to see--

00:04:26.190 --> 00:04:31.620
sensor data or aggregated
data of one device

00:04:31.620 --> 00:04:33.660
for multiple devices.

00:04:33.660 --> 00:04:37.060
And there is a
horizontal layer, here.

00:04:37.060 --> 00:04:39.660
So all these building
blocks are used

00:04:39.660 --> 00:04:43.140
by various users or personas.

00:04:43.140 --> 00:04:50.520
And these users may have very
different access control needs

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:52.210
or limitations.

00:04:52.210 --> 00:04:54.390
And this is going
to be a focus--

00:04:54.390 --> 00:04:59.700
in our talk-- how to implement
authorization for these users.

00:04:59.700 --> 00:05:02.190
Because we have seen
that, many times,

00:05:02.190 --> 00:05:03.894
this is very challenging.

00:05:06.560 --> 00:05:10.720
So, depending on how
the business is run,

00:05:10.720 --> 00:05:13.540
the same building
blocks are used.

00:05:13.540 --> 00:05:16.760
And there are three
main categories here.

00:05:16.760 --> 00:05:19.810
We are going to see
examples for each.

00:05:19.810 --> 00:05:21.700
So B2B, business to business.

00:05:21.700 --> 00:05:25.840
B2B2B-- there is
another B added here--

00:05:25.840 --> 00:05:28.090
and B2B2C.

00:05:28.090 --> 00:05:35.990
And the first B is always the
IoT or Cloud Service provider.

00:05:35.990 --> 00:05:38.110
So in our case,
it's Google Cloud.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.690
And then the second
B is our customer.

00:05:40.690 --> 00:05:44.440
And, in many times, our
customers operate the service--

00:05:44.440 --> 00:05:46.300
some sort of Sas service--

00:05:46.300 --> 00:05:50.230
in IoT Solution, using
our infrastructure.

00:05:50.230 --> 00:05:55.410
And sell these services either
to businesses or consumers.

00:05:58.160 --> 00:06:02.000
So to give you an
example of all of these,

00:06:02.000 --> 00:06:06.380
probably the B2B
scenario is the simplest,

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:08.990
in terms of implementation.

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:12.260
And probably the
best example would

00:06:12.260 --> 00:06:18.980
be a connected factory, or
connected manufacturing line.

00:06:18.980 --> 00:06:23.120
So a factory with
connected PLCs.

00:06:23.120 --> 00:06:27.890
When it comes to implementing
these use-cases--

00:06:27.890 --> 00:06:30.290
based on these business models--

00:06:30.290 --> 00:06:36.470
usually, it's enough to
use the resources provided

00:06:36.470 --> 00:06:39.930
by the Cloud service provider.

00:06:39.930 --> 00:06:42.500
So, case in this
case, Google Cloud.

00:06:42.500 --> 00:06:46.520
So these are existing
resources, or building blocks.

00:06:46.520 --> 00:06:53.180
And implementing these
use-cases can rely only

00:06:53.180 --> 00:06:55.310
on these components.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:07:02.790
And, very importantly, there are
no end users in B2B use cases.

00:07:02.790 --> 00:07:04.970
This is a little bit
more complicated,

00:07:04.970 --> 00:07:09.110
the B2B2B scenario.

00:07:09.110 --> 00:07:13.400
Maybe the best way to describe
this category of use-cases

00:07:13.400 --> 00:07:15.000
is through an example.

00:07:15.000 --> 00:07:20.570
So imagine a connected truck
fleet management system.

00:07:20.570 --> 00:07:25.640
A company that provides this
truck fleet management services

00:07:25.640 --> 00:07:29.000
as excess service, and
let's say they also

00:07:29.000 --> 00:07:33.140
sell hardware that
their customers can

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:36.260
install in their trucks.

00:07:36.260 --> 00:07:39.290
Or it can be other
vehicle, as well.

00:07:39.290 --> 00:07:45.300
And there are end
users in this scenario.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:50.450
So, if you think about the
customers of the Sas provider--

00:07:50.450 --> 00:07:53.930
the connected truck fleet
management system provider--

00:07:53.930 --> 00:07:55.860
those users are end users.

00:07:55.860 --> 00:08:05.600
So for instance, a driver or
operator of a given fleet.

00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:08.880
And we have to realize
that in these use-cases,

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:13.170
there is a really wide spectrum
of roles and permissions.

00:08:13.170 --> 00:08:18.730
So just to reflect on
my previous example,

00:08:18.730 --> 00:08:21.680
a drive has really
different access rights

00:08:21.680 --> 00:08:27.752
compared to the manager
of a whole fleet.

00:08:27.752 --> 00:08:32.210
The B2B2C scenario is
a little bit simpler,

00:08:32.210 --> 00:08:35.840
but similar to the previous one.

00:08:35.840 --> 00:08:39.860
Probably, this is the most
familiar to all of you.

00:08:39.860 --> 00:08:44.000
A good example would be a
smart lighting solution.

00:08:44.000 --> 00:08:45.620
Really, the only
difference here,

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:51.020
is that the end
users are consumers.

00:08:51.020 --> 00:08:55.370
And in these
use-cases, there are

00:08:55.370 --> 00:08:59.360
multiple personas, and roles,
and permission requirements.

00:08:59.360 --> 00:09:01.510
But, typically, less
than in case of B2B2B.

00:09:05.870 --> 00:09:07.790
I wanted to show you
a real world example.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:10.530
And it's very relevant
here, in San Francisco.

00:09:10.530 --> 00:09:14.220
So, you might have seen these
scooters out on the streets.

00:09:14.220 --> 00:09:17.030
These are connected
scooters, so they

00:09:17.030 --> 00:09:18.920
are connected to the internet.

00:09:18.920 --> 00:09:20.930
And there is a
corresponding app,

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.500
or there is an app
you need to use if you

00:09:24.500 --> 00:09:25.790
want to ride the scooters.

00:09:25.790 --> 00:09:31.460
And there is a very
complex business logic

00:09:31.460 --> 00:09:33.580
behind the service.

00:09:33.580 --> 00:09:35.150
So-- if you think about it--

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:38.420
the user of the
application and the scooter

00:09:38.420 --> 00:09:42.680
needs to gain access
rides to the scooter just,

00:09:42.680 --> 00:09:47.300
for the period of that
person is riding the scooter.

00:09:47.300 --> 00:09:52.210
So this is a pretty complex
business logic, here.

00:09:52.210 --> 00:09:57.780
And they are actually using
Google Cloud Services.

00:09:57.780 --> 00:10:04.530
Just to give you a summary of
some of these various business

00:10:04.530 --> 00:10:07.830
models, or sort
of overview them.

00:10:07.830 --> 00:10:11.340
So, in all cases,
the manufacturer

00:10:11.340 --> 00:10:16.170
is some kind of device
maker or an OEM.

00:10:16.170 --> 00:10:21.030
That's common in
all three scenarios.

00:10:21.030 --> 00:10:24.990
When it comes to the
owner of the devices--

00:10:24.990 --> 00:10:26.850
so who owns the device--

00:10:26.850 --> 00:10:29.020
in case of B2B, this is trivial.

00:10:29.020 --> 00:10:33.990
It's called customer of
the Cloud Service Provider,

00:10:33.990 --> 00:10:38.250
so it's a corporate
owner or an operator.

00:10:38.250 --> 00:10:41.370
And there is no end
user in case of B2B.

00:10:41.370 --> 00:10:45.630
In the other two categories,
there are end users.

00:10:45.630 --> 00:10:49.800
In case of B2B2B,
the interesting thing

00:10:49.800 --> 00:10:53.850
is that the owner is
also a corporate owner--

00:10:53.850 --> 00:10:57.000
or operator-- but the
end user is different.

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.270
So they don't necessarily
own the hardware.

00:11:00.270 --> 00:11:06.600
So, just to reflect back on the
truck fleet management system,

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.090
this hardware-- or
these tracking devices--

00:11:09.090 --> 00:11:11.220
might be installed on trucks.

00:11:11.220 --> 00:11:15.000
But let's say the
driver of the truck

00:11:15.000 --> 00:11:18.000
doesn't necessarily
own the device.

00:11:18.000 --> 00:11:22.020
In case of B2B2C, this
is a little bit simpler.

00:11:22.020 --> 00:11:26.310
Because in these use-cases,
usually the owner and the end

00:11:26.310 --> 00:11:28.150
user are the same person.

00:11:28.150 --> 00:11:30.870
So think about the
connected light example.

00:11:34.030 --> 00:11:37.280
All right, so when it comes
to the implementation--

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:41.980
and we will go into the
actual details very soon--

00:11:41.980 --> 00:11:46.480
but just to review, that
there are two main layers

00:11:46.480 --> 00:11:52.570
of the sort of,
back implementation

00:11:52.570 --> 00:11:53.770
of these use-cases.

00:11:53.770 --> 00:11:59.270
Application and infrastructure,
just like in other systems.

00:11:59.270 --> 00:12:01.520
This is true in IoT, as well.

00:12:01.520 --> 00:12:03.490
So what's typical
for infrastructure

00:12:03.490 --> 00:12:07.690
is that usually there
are Cloud Services.

00:12:07.690 --> 00:12:12.430
And these are pre-existing
building blocks.

00:12:12.430 --> 00:12:19.330
So, for instance, like, Compute
Engine instances-- or devices--

00:12:19.330 --> 00:12:22.970
but there are many of
them in Google Cloud.

00:12:22.970 --> 00:12:26.165
What is common is that there
is a service called Cloud IAM.

00:12:26.165 --> 00:12:32.350
And Cloud IAM controls what
sort of permissions a given

00:12:32.350 --> 00:12:36.400
user has on what resources.

00:12:36.400 --> 00:12:41.920
And in this layer, the
resources are obviously

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:44.300
defined by the Cloud
Service Provider.

00:12:44.300 --> 00:12:47.110
So in our case, it's
defined by Google.

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:51.910
And typically, in
case of B2B use-cases,

00:12:51.910 --> 00:12:55.880
the implementation is
heavier in this layer.

00:12:55.880 --> 00:13:00.370
So there is less need
for an application layer.

00:13:00.370 --> 00:13:03.310
Because you can usually use
these pre-existing building

00:13:03.310 --> 00:13:05.670
blocks.

00:13:05.670 --> 00:13:07.120
The application layer.

00:13:07.120 --> 00:13:10.140
This is where the
business logic--

00:13:10.140 --> 00:13:12.030
most of the business logic--

00:13:12.030 --> 00:13:13.410
is implemented.

00:13:13.410 --> 00:13:19.970
This is where the command and
control decisions are made.

00:13:19.970 --> 00:13:24.210
And this is the layer
responsible for transmitting

00:13:24.210 --> 00:13:27.600
these decisions to the
infrastructure layer.

00:13:27.600 --> 00:13:31.590
We can look at this layer as the
digital version of the business

00:13:31.590 --> 00:13:33.730
model, of a given use-case.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:38.580
And this layer is
usually thicker

00:13:38.580 --> 00:13:45.940
in case of the B2B2B
and B2B2C use-cases.

00:13:45.940 --> 00:13:48.880
Now, an interesting
question is that, of course,

00:13:48.880 --> 00:13:52.480
these two layers live together.

00:13:52.480 --> 00:13:59.350
So they are implemented
together and both exist.

00:13:59.350 --> 00:14:04.530
And there is always some sort
of overlap between the two.

00:14:04.530 --> 00:14:08.310
And the question is, how do
we crossover between the two

00:14:08.310 --> 00:14:09.780
layers?

00:14:09.780 --> 00:14:14.310
How do we bridge between
a device that only exists

00:14:14.310 --> 00:14:17.070
as an infrastructure piece?

00:14:17.070 --> 00:14:22.730
And its authorization
is controlled by IAM.

00:14:22.730 --> 00:14:29.160
So how do we provide access to
this device for a user that--

00:14:29.160 --> 00:14:34.980
for instance, only exists in a
third party identity provider--

00:14:34.980 --> 00:14:38.460
which can be even
external to Google.

00:14:38.460 --> 00:14:43.470
And the good news is
that Google has solutions

00:14:43.470 --> 00:14:45.700
to solve these problems.

00:14:45.700 --> 00:14:49.650
And we have tools to implement
these authorisation control

00:14:49.650 --> 00:14:50.850
mechanisms.

00:14:50.850 --> 00:14:53.400
And so I would like to
invite Preston on stage

00:14:53.400 --> 00:14:54.830
to tell more how to do that.

00:14:57.690 --> 00:15:00.650
PRESTON HOLMES:
Thank you, Zoltan.

00:15:00.650 --> 00:15:03.980
So, we talked about
the general cases

00:15:03.980 --> 00:15:07.230
of the infrastructure layer
and the application layer.

00:15:07.230 --> 00:15:10.970
So if we look first at some
of the infrastructure ways

00:15:10.970 --> 00:15:15.380
that we can apply IAM
in Cloud IoT Core.

00:15:15.380 --> 00:15:19.370
Which is our primary service for
authorizing, and identifying,

00:15:19.370 --> 00:15:22.130
and securely connecting
devices that exist out

00:15:22.130 --> 00:15:25.640
in sort of the field to cloud.

00:15:25.640 --> 00:15:28.640
This service has a set of
IAM permissions and roles

00:15:28.640 --> 00:15:31.130
that let you define
who can do what

00:15:31.130 --> 00:15:33.740
with the logical
device resources that

00:15:33.740 --> 00:15:35.930
are stored in IoT Core.

00:15:35.930 --> 00:15:39.270
And, like many cloud
IAM permissions,

00:15:39.270 --> 00:15:42.680
these can be applied at
different scopes of influence.

00:15:42.680 --> 00:15:46.100
So this is an example of
where these permissions can be

00:15:46.100 --> 00:15:48.450
applied to the entire project.

00:15:48.450 --> 00:15:50.630
But inside Cloud
IoT Core, we also

00:15:50.630 --> 00:15:54.140
provide the ability to apply
these permissions-- or rules--

00:15:54.140 --> 00:15:58.950
to specific device registries
or collections within a project.

00:15:58.950 --> 00:16:01.970
So this is something where
you're applying Cloud IAM

00:16:01.970 --> 00:16:05.120
to control who can access--
or who can perform certain

00:16:05.120 --> 00:16:05.630
actions--

00:16:05.630 --> 00:16:09.380
on Cloud resources
defined by Google

00:16:09.380 --> 00:16:11.810
in a managed service
like Cloud Core.

00:16:14.390 --> 00:16:16.850
The other thing
you can use IAM for

00:16:16.850 --> 00:16:20.600
is controlling access to who
can publish to certain Cloud

00:16:20.600 --> 00:16:21.650
Pub/Sub topics.

00:16:21.650 --> 00:16:24.260
So Cloud IoT Core
and Cloud Pub/Sub

00:16:24.260 --> 00:16:26.090
are very tightly integrated.

00:16:26.090 --> 00:16:30.380
And by limiting who can
publish to certain topics,

00:16:30.380 --> 00:16:35.390
you can constrain Pub/Sub
that only messages being

00:16:35.390 --> 00:16:39.710
published by devices to IoT
Core arrive on a Pub/Sub topic.

00:16:39.710 --> 00:16:42.980
And this is important because
that way you can use certain

00:16:42.980 --> 00:16:46.610
attributes published by
IoT Core on messages--

00:16:46.610 --> 00:16:48.470
such as a device ID--

00:16:48.470 --> 00:16:51.110
and know that those
attributes were only

00:16:51.110 --> 00:16:52.280
coming through devices.

00:16:52.280 --> 00:16:56.300
And not through some other
cloud publisher online

00:16:56.300 --> 00:16:58.290
that has access to
that Pub/Sub topic.

00:16:58.290 --> 00:17:02.360
So it's one of the constraints
you can put on other systems

00:17:02.360 --> 00:17:05.210
that IoT Core interacts with.

00:17:05.210 --> 00:17:07.880
Now the title of his
talk is IOT and Firebase.

00:17:07.880 --> 00:17:11.150
And Firebase is actually
a very large suite

00:17:11.150 --> 00:17:13.920
of products and services.

00:17:13.920 --> 00:17:16.250
And we're certainly not going
to cover them all today.

00:17:16.250 --> 00:17:19.310
There is also the fact that a
number of these services now

00:17:19.310 --> 00:17:21.319
sort of have dual
representations

00:17:21.319 --> 00:17:27.353
in both the Firebase experience
and the Cloud GCP experience.

00:17:27.353 --> 00:17:29.270
So the ones where it's
going to be touching on

00:17:29.270 --> 00:17:33.530
ARE authentication hosting
Firestore and Cloud Functions.

00:17:33.530 --> 00:17:37.818
So authentication is the kind
of customer identity management,

00:17:37.818 --> 00:17:39.110
I'll get into that in a second.

00:17:39.110 --> 00:17:40.400
Hosting is static hosting.

00:17:40.400 --> 00:17:44.130
Firestore is a
document database.

00:17:44.130 --> 00:17:47.240
And then Cloud Functions.

00:17:47.240 --> 00:17:49.670
Again, this is the same
back end infrastructure

00:17:49.670 --> 00:17:52.550
shared between Google Cloud
Platform and Firebase,

00:17:52.550 --> 00:17:56.580
as far as executing
lightweight functions.

00:17:56.580 --> 00:17:59.310
So the first is around
identity management.

00:17:59.310 --> 00:18:02.220
So when Zoltan was talking
about the main components,

00:18:02.220 --> 00:18:04.680
this is how you
manage your own users.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:08.090
So when you do have
end users involved,

00:18:08.090 --> 00:18:10.250
you need a way of
managing who they are.

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:13.930
This handles user
authentication,

00:18:13.930 --> 00:18:17.100
recognizing that these
users exist in your system.

00:18:17.100 --> 00:18:20.030
So when a developer logs
into Google Cloud Platform,

00:18:20.030 --> 00:18:22.460
our Google identity
system is what

00:18:22.460 --> 00:18:26.210
we expect infrastructure
users to be able to log into.

00:18:26.210 --> 00:18:28.950
Now, you can integrate
that with AD, et cetera.

00:18:28.950 --> 00:18:31.790
But that's identity
management for infrastructure

00:18:31.790 --> 00:18:34.190
this is identity
management that we

00:18:34.190 --> 00:18:36.290
offer-- as a service--
for you to incorporate

00:18:36.290 --> 00:18:39.440
into your own applications.

00:18:39.440 --> 00:18:41.810
And once you have
authentication--

00:18:41.810 --> 00:18:44.360
then to address authorization--

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:47.600
this is where Firebase rules
that apply to Firestore

00:18:47.600 --> 00:18:48.560
come in.

00:18:48.560 --> 00:18:52.940
And this is a service
that allows applications

00:18:52.940 --> 00:18:55.700
to communicate directly
to the database

00:18:55.700 --> 00:18:59.430
without needing to go through
any sort of API middle tier.

00:18:59.430 --> 00:19:01.820
And if you're going to have
applications talking directly

00:19:01.820 --> 00:19:06.320
to a database, you need to
apply authorization logic.

00:19:06.320 --> 00:19:09.890
And the way we do that is a we
have a service that basically

00:19:09.890 --> 00:19:12.320
lets you describe
that authorization

00:19:12.320 --> 00:19:14.210
syntax in your application.

00:19:14.210 --> 00:19:18.050
And have the database
actually apply that for you.

00:19:18.050 --> 00:19:20.300
So we'll get a little bit
more into some of the syntax

00:19:20.300 --> 00:19:22.310
for these rules a little.

00:19:22.310 --> 00:19:26.360
Later but let's talk about
the kind of architecture

00:19:26.360 --> 00:19:28.190
we're going to show
and demonstrate, here.

00:19:28.190 --> 00:19:32.180
And that is we've got
some devices on the left.

00:19:32.180 --> 00:19:34.730
These are, kind of,
hypothetical-- sort of,

00:19:34.730 --> 00:19:36.800
gas sensors in this
case-- we're going to use.

00:19:36.800 --> 00:19:40.880
And then users end users
interacting with an application

00:19:40.880 --> 00:19:42.385
on the right.

00:19:42.385 --> 00:19:43.760
So the first thing
we do is we're

00:19:43.760 --> 00:19:46.280
going to use Cloud IoT
Core to securely connect

00:19:46.280 --> 00:19:49.160
the devices to the cloud.

00:19:49.160 --> 00:19:50.670
And then on the
application side,

00:19:50.670 --> 00:19:53.690
we're going to host
the static content

00:19:53.690 --> 00:19:55.760
related to the application.

00:19:55.760 --> 00:19:59.290
And deliver that to
users in an browsers

00:19:59.290 --> 00:20:02.890
or mobile applications
from Firebase Hosting.

00:20:02.890 --> 00:20:05.343
And then the live data
that the application

00:20:05.343 --> 00:20:06.760
is going to be
interacting with is

00:20:06.760 --> 00:20:08.770
going to come from Firestore.

00:20:08.770 --> 00:20:10.830
But because Firestore
is directly connected

00:20:10.830 --> 00:20:13.330
to the application, we're going
to make sure that fire still

00:20:13.330 --> 00:20:18.280
rules are applied to the way
the application is getting data

00:20:18.280 --> 00:20:20.170
in and out of Firestore.

00:20:20.170 --> 00:20:23.550
Now to connect these two
for device sensor data,

00:20:23.550 --> 00:20:26.140
we're going to use the
Cloud IoT Core's integration

00:20:26.140 --> 00:20:27.010
with Pub/Sub.

00:20:27.010 --> 00:20:30.760
And then Cloud Functions that
take readings from sensors,

00:20:30.760 --> 00:20:33.700
and write them into Firestore.

00:20:33.700 --> 00:20:37.330
We also could have chosen
other cloud platform database

00:20:37.330 --> 00:20:38.210
products to do this.

00:20:38.210 --> 00:20:40.335
For example, we could write
this sensor series data

00:20:40.335 --> 00:20:42.370
in the Cloud Bigtable.

00:20:42.370 --> 00:20:45.407
Very scalable way of doing
series data, to be honest.

00:20:45.407 --> 00:20:46.990
In this case, it
would have also meant

00:20:46.990 --> 00:20:50.710
we would have had to
replace the Firestore

00:20:50.710 --> 00:20:54.830
rules with our own
authorization implementation.

00:20:54.830 --> 00:20:56.830
So we're going to go with
the simple path, here.

00:20:56.830 --> 00:20:59.230
And just use Firestore
because it gives us

00:20:59.230 --> 00:21:01.990
that rules sort of value.

00:21:01.990 --> 00:21:03.730
We're also going
to send information

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:06.850
from the device in the Firestore
about the current settings

00:21:06.850 --> 00:21:08.900
and state of the device.

00:21:08.900 --> 00:21:10.660
So this isn't necessarily
the sensor data.

00:21:10.660 --> 00:21:14.170
But this might be other
current settings and values

00:21:14.170 --> 00:21:17.530
that are on the device,
changing less often.

00:21:17.530 --> 00:21:21.070
And then finally when a user
changes a setting in a device,

00:21:21.070 --> 00:21:22.910
we need to get that
back out to the device.

00:21:22.910 --> 00:21:27.070
So, again, we use a
Cloud Firestore trigger

00:21:27.070 --> 00:21:30.160
to run a Cloud Function when
data changes in Firestore.

00:21:30.160 --> 00:21:31.720
And that Cloud
Function, then, can

00:21:31.720 --> 00:21:36.325
send the updated configuration
through IoT Core to the device.

00:21:38.980 --> 00:21:40.800
So when you put it
all together, it

00:21:40.800 --> 00:21:42.850
looks a little
something like this.

00:21:42.850 --> 00:21:44.490
So what we're really
trying to leverage

00:21:44.490 --> 00:21:48.930
here is using as many
server and managed services.

00:21:48.930 --> 00:21:52.900
We've got direction data
flowing in both directions.

00:21:52.900 --> 00:21:55.980
And then we've got static
content and data being served

00:21:55.980 --> 00:21:59.530
to an end user application.

00:21:59.530 --> 00:22:03.000
So the scenario for this
demo is going to be sort of,

00:22:03.000 --> 00:22:06.390
a hypothetical technician
who needs to check out

00:22:06.390 --> 00:22:09.270
a piece of equipment.

00:22:09.270 --> 00:22:14.557
And here is my gas sensor
that we're going to use.

00:22:14.557 --> 00:22:16.140
And what they're
going to end up doing

00:22:16.140 --> 00:22:20.320
is connect to it
through a mobile app.

00:22:20.320 --> 00:22:22.950
So I'm going to test the
demo gods, because I've

00:22:22.950 --> 00:22:27.300
got an IoT device, a
tablet, and a demo laptop

00:22:27.300 --> 00:22:32.370
all concurrently interacting
with our Wi-Fi saturated world.

00:22:32.370 --> 00:22:35.615
So let me plug into this.

00:22:41.290 --> 00:22:43.530
So if we can have
the demo system up.

00:22:48.480 --> 00:22:49.470
Got it.

00:22:52.450 --> 00:22:56.330
All right, we can go ahead.

00:22:56.330 --> 00:22:59.050
I'm going to restart
the casting here.

00:23:03.370 --> 00:23:07.190
So we can have the
look that has the--

00:23:07.190 --> 00:23:08.190
and reconnect.

00:23:10.700 --> 00:23:14.300
The AV guys loved our
requirements list for this one.

00:23:19.190 --> 00:23:21.440
All right, so here's the tablet.

00:23:21.440 --> 00:23:23.550
I'm going to launch this app.

00:23:23.550 --> 00:23:25.760
So the first thing is, let
me go ahead and back out.

00:23:25.760 --> 00:23:26.635
I'm going to log out.

00:23:26.635 --> 00:23:30.140
This app really doesn't do
anything until you log in.

00:23:30.140 --> 00:23:32.630
So when I log in with Google--

00:23:32.630 --> 00:23:36.170
actually, maybe I'll just
keep it that way, honestly.

00:23:36.170 --> 00:23:39.870
When I log in with Google, I've
already authorized this app.

00:23:39.870 --> 00:23:42.110
So directly using it to
go to a list of devices

00:23:42.110 --> 00:23:45.580
that are associated with me.

00:23:45.580 --> 00:23:47.330
This is a new device,
so I've just kind of

00:23:47.330 --> 00:23:49.390
checked this out of a
rack, say, a shared rack

00:23:49.390 --> 00:23:50.720
at the technician.

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:54.470
And what's displayed on here,
is a code that says let me--

00:23:54.470 --> 00:23:56.360
as an employee--
claim this device

00:23:56.360 --> 00:23:59.760
so that I have sort of
exclusive control over it.

00:23:59.760 --> 00:24:09.460
So if I add this claim ID,
and it's my favorite one

00:24:09.460 --> 00:24:12.720
so I'm just going
to name it fave.

00:24:12.720 --> 00:24:16.900
Most of you won't be able to see
this change but I'll hit claim.

00:24:16.900 --> 00:24:20.580
And as long as the network is
still good and connect it here,

00:24:20.580 --> 00:24:21.810
this now says claimed.

00:24:21.810 --> 00:24:27.420
So the device has now
recognized that it's claimed.

00:24:27.420 --> 00:24:29.610
So I'm going to go into
the detail view, here.

00:24:29.610 --> 00:24:33.210
So when you see on that real
time live graph is Light Data--

00:24:33.210 --> 00:24:36.360
Light Sensor Data--
coming from this device.

00:24:36.360 --> 00:24:38.250
And that's coming through
that telemetry path

00:24:38.250 --> 00:24:40.500
that I showed in
the architecture.

00:24:40.500 --> 00:24:44.310
You also can see that there
are a set of configuration

00:24:44.310 --> 00:24:46.120
settings, and that
is a tilt threshold.

00:24:46.120 --> 00:24:48.105
And so what that
means is, I have

00:24:48.105 --> 00:24:52.290
an angle set that if I tilt this
device past a certain range,

00:24:52.290 --> 00:24:54.260
I get a beep.

00:24:54.260 --> 00:24:56.240
Now as a user, if
I want to change

00:24:56.240 --> 00:24:58.760
the tolerance of those tilt
settings, I can go ahead.

00:24:58.760 --> 00:25:02.240
And in this app, I'm going to
narrow those down, let me just

00:25:02.240 --> 00:25:04.147
actually narrow one side here.

00:25:04.147 --> 00:25:06.230
Now when I hit Update, you
see a little spinner up

00:25:06.230 --> 00:25:08.602
in the dialog?

00:25:08.602 --> 00:25:10.310
That's actually letting
me know that it's

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:13.430
taken a moment for the settings
to get to the device and then

00:25:13.430 --> 00:25:15.680
the device--

00:25:15.680 --> 00:25:17.287
it's doing that again.

00:25:17.287 --> 00:25:19.870
Well what you can see here, is,
I've narrowed the tilt setting

00:25:19.870 --> 00:25:23.410
and now it's a lot more
sensitive, all right?

00:25:23.410 --> 00:25:25.480
Let me see, we'll
go one more time.

00:25:25.480 --> 00:25:27.430
We have enough time that I can--

00:25:27.430 --> 00:25:31.310
if you bear with me we'll--

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:33.170
this is a relatively
older tablet

00:25:33.170 --> 00:25:35.730
that I pulled out for
this particular thing.

00:25:35.730 --> 00:25:37.790
So I think between
us and the stage.

00:25:41.570 --> 00:25:45.030
So before it glitches again,
I'll do this one more time.

00:25:45.030 --> 00:25:48.382
I'll change the setting
and I'll update it.

00:25:48.382 --> 00:25:50.090
In fact, I'll do it
again, one more time.

00:25:50.090 --> 00:25:57.140
I'll narrow this other
direction of tilt. Hopefully

00:25:57.140 --> 00:25:59.910
I haven't put it
into a state of here.

00:25:59.910 --> 00:26:02.940
We'll see here that
now, if I tilt this--

00:26:02.940 --> 00:26:04.170
essentially what I'm tilting.

00:26:04.170 --> 00:26:05.940
But what I want to
do is show you back

00:26:05.940 --> 00:26:16.660
on the laptop, a little bit
about what's going on here.

00:26:16.660 --> 00:26:19.030
So that progress
spinner is actually

00:26:19.030 --> 00:26:22.240
giving the user of the
tablet an indication

00:26:22.240 --> 00:26:26.050
that the settings have not
yet actually been verified

00:26:26.050 --> 00:26:28.180
as received by the device.

00:26:28.180 --> 00:26:32.480
So I change a
setting in the UI, I

00:26:32.480 --> 00:26:36.470
update the setting that changes
data in Cloud Firestore.

00:26:36.470 --> 00:26:39.200
That change of data
in Cloud Firestore

00:26:39.200 --> 00:26:43.580
triggers a Cloud Function to run
and send the new settings down

00:26:43.580 --> 00:26:47.040
to the device through IoT Core.

00:26:47.040 --> 00:26:48.870
When the device
receives the settings

00:26:48.870 --> 00:26:51.070
and applies them
to the tilt sensor,

00:26:51.070 --> 00:26:54.480
that sends another state
update message back

00:26:54.480 --> 00:26:57.360
through that other top pathway.

00:26:57.360 --> 00:27:01.920
Which confirms the state
as updated in Firestore.

00:27:01.920 --> 00:27:05.520
And only when do those
two data values reconcile,

00:27:05.520 --> 00:27:07.630
does the spinner go away.

00:27:07.630 --> 00:27:10.740
So when you saw me update
a setting in the tablet--

00:27:10.740 --> 00:27:15.150
and you saw a spinner
for a moment--

00:27:15.150 --> 00:27:16.770
that's actually a
sort of a full round

00:27:16.770 --> 00:27:19.440
trip to the device and back,
confirming those settings.

00:27:22.075 --> 00:27:23.700
I'm going to show
you a little bit more

00:27:23.700 --> 00:27:27.240
about how these
Firebase rules apply.

00:27:27.240 --> 00:27:32.940
Because these rules are what are
preventing one user from seeing

00:27:32.940 --> 00:27:36.270
data from a device-- or
changing settings of a device--

00:27:36.270 --> 00:27:41.350
while another user has
that device claimed.

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:47.180
And the syntax of these rules is
based around matching patterns

00:27:47.180 --> 00:27:49.970
of a document in Firestore.

00:27:49.970 --> 00:27:55.310
So, first of all, the outcome
of a rule is to allow--

00:27:55.310 --> 00:27:57.870
or not allow-- a given device.

00:27:57.870 --> 00:28:00.440
So, essentially, it's
denied by default.

00:28:00.440 --> 00:28:04.280
And unless a matching
rule is found,

00:28:04.280 --> 00:28:07.610
don't allow the user to
actually read or write data

00:28:07.610 --> 00:28:09.120
from the database.

00:28:09.120 --> 00:28:11.990
So you can see the operations
can be read or write.

00:28:11.990 --> 00:28:13.745
There's actually also
update and delete.

00:28:16.340 --> 00:28:18.710
And the main one I
want to point out

00:28:18.710 --> 00:28:21.980
is the kind of
middle section, here.

00:28:21.980 --> 00:28:27.500
So I'll go from the top, which
is that the first rule is

00:28:27.500 --> 00:28:31.640
around letting a user basically,
update their own user's

00:28:31.640 --> 00:28:32.540
profile, right?

00:28:32.540 --> 00:28:37.460
And that makes sense unless the
request coming from the user

00:28:37.460 --> 00:28:39.620
matches the profile
user's ID, you

00:28:39.620 --> 00:28:42.270
can't update somebody
else's profile.

00:28:42.270 --> 00:28:48.210
The second one is related to
the device record, itself.

00:28:48.210 --> 00:28:50.570
So, in this case, we
want to allow a person

00:28:50.570 --> 00:28:54.017
to create a new
record, if they're

00:28:54.017 --> 00:28:55.850
going to be the first
to claim a device that

00:28:55.850 --> 00:28:57.620
can have that entered.

00:28:57.620 --> 00:29:00.890
They also can read and write
the settings for a given device.

00:29:00.890 --> 00:29:03.080
Because that's where
the updating of settings

00:29:03.080 --> 00:29:05.240
is being written to.

00:29:05.240 --> 00:29:07.140
And then the telemetry path.

00:29:07.140 --> 00:29:09.500
This is where we are
storing incoming sensors.

00:29:09.500 --> 00:29:11.990
So that real time
line graph was going

00:29:11.990 --> 00:29:14.480
to a telemetry collection.

00:29:14.480 --> 00:29:17.960
Just to backup and give a little
bit more context for those

00:29:17.960 --> 00:29:21.350
who don't know as much on
the Firestore of the document

00:29:21.350 --> 00:29:25.260
database, documents are
organized into collection.

00:29:25.260 --> 00:29:28.190
So, here, we're talking about
three different collections;

00:29:28.190 --> 00:29:31.580
users, devices, and telemetry.

00:29:31.580 --> 00:29:34.100
And the way Firestore
rules are applied,

00:29:34.100 --> 00:29:38.060
is you can apply them only to
documents and collections that

00:29:38.060 --> 00:29:40.880
match certain patterns.

00:29:40.880 --> 00:29:42.440
In the case of the
middle one, here,

00:29:42.440 --> 00:29:47.010
what we're doing is we're taking
the device ID-- which is also,

00:29:47.010 --> 00:29:49.130
in this case, the document ID--

00:29:49.130 --> 00:29:52.580
and using that as a
variable in the rule.

00:29:57.370 --> 00:30:01.650
And just to show
you the way this

00:30:01.650 --> 00:30:07.810
works from at the
actual database layer,

00:30:07.810 --> 00:30:12.990
I have another user logged
in to the same system.

00:30:12.990 --> 00:30:18.570
And since the URL path was
used for getting the data

00:30:18.570 --> 00:30:19.620
from that device--

00:30:19.620 --> 00:30:24.180
if I try to put in
that device URL--

00:30:24.180 --> 00:30:28.140
you'll see that not only is it
being blocked at the web front

00:30:28.140 --> 00:30:32.270
end logic, but if you look
at the developer console,

00:30:32.270 --> 00:30:35.260
the permission error is actually
coming from the database,

00:30:35.260 --> 00:30:35.760
right?

00:30:35.760 --> 00:30:38.708
So if I didn't show you
this developer console,

00:30:38.708 --> 00:30:40.500
it'd be very easy to
sort of say, oh, well,

00:30:40.500 --> 00:30:44.220
this is authorization logic
happening in the application

00:30:44.220 --> 00:30:45.540
in the front end.

00:30:45.540 --> 00:30:49.680
That is subject to bugs,
and errors, and in the way

00:30:49.680 --> 00:30:51.410
authorization is applied.

00:30:51.410 --> 00:30:56.700
So, here, by seeing
the developer console,

00:30:56.700 --> 00:30:58.200
I can show you that
this is actually

00:30:58.200 --> 00:31:01.800
being enforced at the
Firebase client library

00:31:01.800 --> 00:31:03.600
and back end integration point.

00:31:06.492 --> 00:31:07.950
We don't need to
put the tablet up,

00:31:07.950 --> 00:31:14.170
but I will go ahead and release
this device from this user.

00:31:19.800 --> 00:31:24.090
And if I go back I
can now claim this.

00:31:33.240 --> 00:31:34.530
And now I can see it.

00:31:34.530 --> 00:31:38.820
And now I can actually get to
that URL for the detail view.

00:31:38.820 --> 00:31:40.680
So that's how we're
able to use sort of,

00:31:40.680 --> 00:31:45.480
the real time nature
Firestore to basically,

00:31:45.480 --> 00:31:49.410
apply these rules based on the
data that's in the database.

00:31:49.410 --> 00:31:52.380
I think that's the other key
takeaway for Firestore rules,

00:31:52.380 --> 00:31:56.040
is that they are
applied per request--

00:31:56.040 --> 00:32:00.570
per document-- in a way
that lets your own rules

00:32:00.570 --> 00:32:03.300
and your own data
models in the database

00:32:03.300 --> 00:32:07.090
be that custom application
layer of authorization.

00:32:07.090 --> 00:32:12.600
So while IAM has a focus on
the resources-- or the models--

00:32:12.600 --> 00:32:17.730
that are defined by
Google, if you're

00:32:17.730 --> 00:32:21.900
defining the sort of application
objects in Firestore,

00:32:21.900 --> 00:32:24.900
you can apply Firestore
rules in a custom way

00:32:24.900 --> 00:32:27.330
to those objects in that data.

00:32:27.330 --> 00:32:30.680
